                      (    forth6809.asm):07632                                   ; count 0), with one enclosing DO and no
                      (    forth6809.asm):07633                                   ; prior UNLOOP (discards the full 8-byte
                      (    forth6809.asm):07634                                   ; frame correctly). The one combination that
                      (    forth6809.asm):07635                                   ; broke was UNLOOP immediately before EXIT -
                      (    forth6809.asm):07636                                   ; UNLOOP discarding 6 of the 8 bytes itself,
                      (    forth6809.asm):07637                                   ; then EXITUNLOOP unconditionally discarding
                      (    forth6809.asm):07638                                   ; a full 8 more, overshooting into whatever
                      (    forth6809.asm):07639                                   ; sat below (typically the caller's own
                      (    forth6809.asm):07640                                   ; return address) and branching into random
                      (    forth6809.asm):07641                                   ; memory on return. Since UNLOOP has no
                      (    forth6809.asm):07642                                   ; other legitimate use than immediately
                      (    forth6809.asm):07643                                   ; preceding an exit from the definition, and
                      (    forth6809.asm):07644                                   ; EXIT already handles that correctly on its
                      (    forth6809.asm):07645                                   ; own, UNLOOP is now a true no-op rather than
                      (    forth6809.asm):07646                                   ; a second, conflicting discard mechanism.
